{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Problem\n\nAfter a reclaim publish-swap, a clone whose `origin/tickets` was already fetched to the rewritten baseline refuses every ticket write as DIVERGED, even though the remote's store epoch provably supersedes the local one and `_store/sync.py::_override_if_reclaimed` would move the clone onto the new history.\n\nObserved 2026-11-20 PST after publishing 6a6dda16fc7: the box MCP server's first write (`comment_ticket`) failed with \"ticket write refused: the local ticket store has DIVERGED from the shared store with no shared ancestor\". The same write through the local CLI succeeded and the sync logged \"clone moved onto reclaimed remote … replayed 0 unpushed event(s)\". The MCP clone had to be reset by hand.\n\n## Cause (reproduced)\n\nTwo defects, both reproduced in a throwaway repo with a real bare origin:\n\n1. `_commands/_seam.py::finalize_event` calls `_refuse_unpublishable_store` → `_store/freshness.py::write_blocking_divergence`, which returns `diverged` whenever `merge-base HEAD <remote>/<branch>` fails. It never consults `compat.remote_epoch_supersedes_local`.\n2. Even when the precheck lets the write through, the event never reaches the remote: the push is rejected as non-fast-forward, and `_store/push_recovery.py::_merge_remote_under_lock` tries to merge the unrelated reclaimed history (5 attempts, each \"merge conflict\"). Only `sync._do_reconverge` knows the stale-clone override; the push recovery path does not.\n\nWhy the CLI write healed and the MCP write did not: every write reaches `sync.reconverge` (fetch + override) only through the read path's `reads.ensure_fresh`, which the CLI runs before every write arm and the library write runs via `emit_cross_session_warning` → `show_ticket`. That reconverge is throttled to once per 60 s by a marker file. A short-lived CLI process on a host whose marker is old reconverges first and is moved onto the reclaimed baseline before `finalize_event` runs. A long-running MCP server that reads constantly sits inside the 60 s window, so its write skips the reconverge and the precheck is the first code to see the already-fetched superseded ref. Reproduction: with the marker stamped as just-run, `rebar.comment` refuses with DIVERGED on current main; with the marker absent, the same write succeeds. What fetched the box's ref without the override running (for example an idle-sync round that found the write lock busy) was not established from the box.\n\n## Approach\n\n- `write_blocking_divergence` returns `None` when `compat.remote_epoch_supersedes_local(tracker, <remote_ref commit>)` is true, so the write commits.\n- `push_recovery._merge_remote_under_lock`, which already holds the store write lock, takes the same stale-clone override as sync when the fetched remote shares no ancestor with HEAD and its epoch supersedes the local one: back up the tip, reset to the reclaimed baseline, replay the fresh unpushed event byte-identically, and report a clean recovery so the next push attempt delivers it. The override is reached through the existing sync helper (made public), so sync and push share one implementation. An override that fails records `store-epoch-pre-merge` and stops, as an epoch refusal does today.\n- No new config key, env var or flag.\n\n## Scope\n\nIn: `src/rebar/_store/freshness.py`, `src/rebar/_store/push_recovery.py`, `src/rebar/_store/sync.py` (expose the existing override helper), and a new regression test module. Out: gate-read freshness (`store_freshness`), which still reports a superseded clone as diverged until a write or sync moves it; changing the 60 s read throttle.\n\n## Testing\n\nNew `tests/interfaces/store/test_write_after_reclaim.py` drives `rebar.comment` against a real bare origin that a parentless baseline replaced, with the read-path throttle marker stamped as just-run so the write precheck is the first code to see the superseded ref. One test publishes a baseline whose lineage names the clone's epoch and asserts the comment lands on the remote tip on top of that baseline; it fails on current main. A second publishes a baseline whose lineage names another epoch and asserts the write is refused as DIVERGED with neither the clone nor the remote moved. Existing `tests/unit/test_stale_clone_override.py`, `tests/unit/test_gate_store_freshness.py`, `tests/interfaces/store/test_fsck_push_pending.py` and the push-recovery tests must stay green, then `make verify`.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] A regression test builds a clone on epoch A, a remote rewritten to epoch B whose lineage names A, fetches the remote ref, and performs a ticket write through the shared seam: the write succeeds and the event is on the remote tip. The test fails on current main.\n- [ ] A remote with an unrelated history (lineage does not name the local epoch) is still refused as DIVERGED.\n- [ ] `make verify` passes."}},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1795233499300529001,"uuid":"d4867d55-5417-4ad4-af41-19bfbe1fe627"}